    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Took its present form in 1839, when William I accepted Belgian independence; the 1848 constitution established parliamentary government.</li><li>Its wealth long rested on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, exploited under the cultivation system and lost when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> won independence in 1949.</li><li>Occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1940–45, it became a founding member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a> and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Since 2010 the Kingdom of the Netherlands has consisted of four countries, the Netherlands, Aruba, Curaçao and Sint Maarten, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonaire">Bonaire</a>, Sint Eustatius and Saba are special <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> municipalities.</p></aside>
    </section>

