    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amsterdam.jpg" alt="Amsterdam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imagen_de_los_canales_conc%C3%A9ntricos_en_%C3%81msterdam.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrés Barrios, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of the Netherlands in its present form dates from 1839, when King William I accepted the loss of the southern provinces that had rebelled in 1830 and recognised Belgian independence in the Treaty of London. The kingdom itself had been created in 1815, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> united the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, which the French had turned into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-holland">Kingdom of Holland</a>, with the former Austrian Netherlands under William I of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a>. For nine years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> William refused to accept the separation, keeping an expensive army mobilised until debts and public discontent forced him to give way. The settlement of 1839 left the northern kingdom with the provinces of the old Republic and the eastern half of Limburg, which was also a member of the German Confederation until 1866, while Luxembourg remained in personal union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> crown until 1890. A constitutional revision in 1840 reduced the king’s power over finance, and William I, unpopular and set on marrying a Belgian Catholic countess, abdicated that year in favour of his son William II. The country’s first railway, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>, had opened in 1839, symbolising a slow turn towards modernisation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nieuwe-waterweg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nieuwe-waterweg.jpg" alt="Nieuwe Waterweg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nieuwe-waterweg">Nieuwe Waterweg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maeslantkering.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DeltaWorks Online - Eszter Simonfi, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the mid-nineteenth century the Netherlands was a trading and agricultural country that industrialised later than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, its prosperity resting on commerce, shipping, dairy farming and, above all, colonial revenue. Under the cultivation system introduced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in 1830 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-van-den-bosch">Johannes van den Bosch</a>, villagers had to grow export crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> for the government, and the profits supplied a large share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> state revenue until the system was dismantled after 1870. Industry expanded from the late nineteenth century: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nieuwe-waterweg">Nieuwe Waterweg</a> of 1872 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea-canal">North Sea Canal</a> of 1876 opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> to large ships, and firms such as Royal Dutch Shell, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philips">Philips</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unilever">Unilever</a> became multinational giants. Society was organised in ‘pillars’ (<em>zuilen</em>) of Protestants, Catholics, socialists and liberals, each with its own parties, unions, schools, newspapers and broadcasters, a system that lasted until the 1960s. Reclamation continued, most ambitiously in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuiderzee-works">Zuiderzee Works</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsluitdijk">Afsluitdijk</a> closed off the inland sea in 1932. After 1945 the country built a generous welfare state, beginning with the state old-age pension of 1957, and the discovery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> natural gas field in 1959 brought great revenues. Rotterdam became for decades the world’s busiest port, and the Netherlands one of the leading trading nations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea.jpg" alt="Battle of the Java Sea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea">Battle of the Java Sea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haguro-1942.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After 1839 the Netherlands pursued a policy of neutrality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, relying on the defensive inundation lines of Holland, the ‘Water Line’, and the fortifications around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. Its wars were fought mainly in the colonies by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-netherlands-east-indies-army">Royal Netherlands East Indies Army</a> (KNIL), above all the long and costly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh-war">Aceh War</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> from 1873, which lasted into the early twentieth century, and campaigns that brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> and other islands under direct rule. The country stayed neutral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, mobilising its army throughout and later granting asylum to the deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Kaiser Wilhelm II</a>. On 10 May 1940 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded without warning; after the bombing of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a> on 14 May and the threat of similar attacks on other cities, the army capitulated on 15 May. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karel-doorman">Karel Doorman</a> was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea">Battle of the Java Sea</a> in February 1942 and the colony surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in March. After 1945 the Netherlands fought to restore its rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, launching two large ‘police actions’ in 1947 and 1948, which international pressure brought to an end. It joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> as a founding member, sent a battalion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and in 1995 its peacekeepers failed to prevent the massacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a>, a failure that led to the government’s resignation in 2002.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/johan-rudolf-thorbecke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-rudolf-thorbecke.jpg" alt="Johan Rudolf Thorbecke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/johan-rudolf-thorbecke">Johan Rudolf Thorbecke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johan_Heinrich_Neuman_-_Johan_Rudolf_Thorbecke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johan Heinrich Neuman, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitutional revision of 1848, drafted by a commission led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-rudolf-thorbecke">Johan Rudolf Thorbecke</a> and accepted by William II amid the revolutions of that year, introduced ministerial responsibility, direct elections to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-representatives-netherlands">Second Chamber</a>, and freedom of assembly, education and religion. Thorbecke led three liberal cabinets, and a conflict in 1866–68 established that a cabinet must have the confidence of parliament. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-the-netherlands">William III</a> (1849–1890) disliked these limits but could not reverse them. The suffrage was extended gradually, and the Pacification of 1917 combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a> and proportional representation with equal state funding for religious schools, ending the long ‘school struggle’; women gained the vote in 1919. Politics was dominated by confessional parties, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-kuyper">Abraham Kuyper</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-revolutionary-party">Anti-Revolutionary Party</a>, and since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> by coalition governments of Christian democrats, social democrats and liberals, such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willem-drees">Willem Drees</a> and, later, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruud-lubbers">Ruud Lubbers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wim-kok">Wim Kok</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-rutte">Mark Rutte</a>. Since 1890 the throne has passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmina-of-the-netherlands">Wilhelmina</a> (1890–1948), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-the-netherlands">Juliana</a> (1948–1980), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatrix-of-the-netherlands">Beatrix</a> (1980–2013) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willem-alexander-of-the-netherlands">Willem-Alexander</a>. The Charter for the Kingdom of 1954 made the Netherlands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and the Netherlands Antilles equal partners in one kingdom, and since 2010 the Kingdom has consisted of the Netherlands, Aruba, Curaçao and Sint Maarten.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/the-hague"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-hague.jpg" alt="The Hague" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Den_Haag_Skyline_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zairon, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As a colonial power the Netherlands ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, one of the largest and most profitable colonies in the world, with a population of some 60 million by 1930, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> islands. Its exploitation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> under the cultivation system was attacked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multatuli">Multatuli</a>’s novel <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-havelaar">Max Havelaar</a></em> (1860), and the ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethical-policy">Ethical Policy</a>’ announced in 1901 promised welfare and education, which in turn helped form the Indonesian nationalist movement. The country’s experience of war left lasting marks: about 75 per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of the Netherlands were murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, one of the highest proportions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, and the diary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-frank">Anne Frank</a>, written in hiding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, became one of the most widely read books in the world. After 1945 the Netherlands became a founding member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benelux">Benelux</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, the European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a> Community and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty">Treaty of Maastricht</a> of 1992 that created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> was signed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> soil. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>, home to the Peace Palace, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> and the International Criminal Court. Dutch water engineering, social policies such as the first legal same-sex marriages in 2001, and artists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh">Vincent van Gogh</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-mondrian">Piet Mondrian</a> have had worldwide influence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pim-fortuyn"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pim-fortuyn.jpg" alt="Pim Fortuyn" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pim-fortuyn">Pim Fortuyn</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pim_Fortuyn_-_May_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roy Beusker, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom has not ended, but it has been transformed from a colonial empire into a European welfare state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> declared independence on 17 August 1945, and after four years of war and negotiation the Netherlands transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> on 27 December 1949; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/netherlands-new-guinea">Netherlands New Guinea</a> followed in 1962–63 through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> became independent in 1975. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> flood of 1953, which killed 1,836 people, led to the Delta Works, a system of dams and storm-surge barriers completed in the 1990s. Pillarisation broke down from the 1960s, as secularisation and protest movements remade a once conservative society, and in 1980 the three main Christian parties merged into the Christian Democratic Appeal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassenaar-agreement">Wassenaar Agreement</a> of 1982 between employers and unions began the consensual ‘polder model’ credited with economic recovery in the 1990s. Since around 2000, debates over immigration and integration, sharpened by the murders of the politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pim-fortuyn">Pim Fortuyn</a> in 2002 and the film-maker <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theo-van-gogh-film-director">Theo van Gogh</a> in 2004, have reshaped party politics, and gas production at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> was wound down after it caused earthquakes. Today the Netherlands is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> of about 18 million people under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willem-alexander-of-the-netherlands">King Willem-Alexander</a>, a member of the euro area and one of the world’s largest exporters, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> as its capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> as the seat of government.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of the Netherlands turned a small trading nation with a vast Asian colony into a model of consensual parliamentary government and of the welfare state, survived German occupation and the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and became a founding pillar of European integration and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>.</em></p>

